---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { ports, manifest, thresholds } = data;
const n = (v: number, d = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });

// From the export, not restated here: the page has to explain an absence, and a caption naming a
// threshold that has since changed is worse than no caption.
const FOR_MEDIAN = thresholds.minimumCallsForMedian;
const FOR_PERCENTILE = thresholds.minimumCallsForPercentile;
const PLAUSIBLY_AT_PORT_NM = thresholds.plausiblyAtPortNm;

const attributed = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.attributedCalls, 0);
const usable = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.usableCalls, 0);
const incomplete = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.excludedIncomplete, 0);
const tooFar = ports.reduce((t, p) => t + p.excludedTooFar, 0);

const ranked = [...ports].sort((a, b) => b.usableCalls - a.usableCalls);
const withAMedian = ports.filter((p) => p.medianWaitingHours !== null).length;

const window = `${manifest.firstFixUtc.slice(0, 10)} to ${manifest.lastFixUtc.slice(0, 10)}`;
---
<Base
  title="Port waits — how long calls actually took, and the sample behind every figure"
  description="Median and 90th-percentile waiting times per port, derived from AIS, each published with the number of calls it rests on and the calls excluded to get there."
>
  <h1>Port waits</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    Not "what is the median" but "was mine unusual". Every figure here carries the sample it rests
    on and what was thrown away to get there.
  </p>

  <ul class="stats">
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(attributed)}</b><span>calls attributed to a port</span></li>
    <li class="stat ok"><b>{n(usable)}</b><span>usable for a benchmark</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{withAMedian}</b><span>ports with enough for a median</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{ports.length}</b><span>ports seen at all</span></li>
  </ul>

  <p class="note prose">
    <strong>{n(attributed)} attributed = {n(usable)} usable + {n(incomplete)} with an unknown
    extent + {n(tooFar)} further than {n(PLAUSIBLY_AT_PORT_NM, 1)} nm from the port named.</strong>
    {' '}Every attributed call is accounted for by exactly one outcome. A call whose extent is unknown
    has hours that are lower bounds, and averaging a bound would understate the wait; a call
    nearest a port but well offshore was at an anchorage, not at that berth.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>port</th><th>country</th>
          <th class="n">attributed</th><th class="n">usable</th>
          <th class="n">open</th><th class="n">too far</th>
          <th class="n">median wait h</th><th class="n">p90 wait h</th><th class="n">median work h</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ranked.map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.portName}</td>
            <td>{p.country}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(p.attributedCalls)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(p.usableCalls)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(p.excludedIncomplete)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(p.excludedTooFar)}</td>
            <td class="n">
              {p.medianWaitingHours === null
                ? <span class="too-few" title={`needs ${FOR_MEDIAN} usable calls`}>too few</span>
                : n(p.medianWaitingHours, 1)}
            </td>
            <td class="n">
              {p.p90WaitingHours === null
                ? <span class="too-few" title={`needs ${FOR_PERCENTILE} usable calls`}>too few</span>
                : n(p.p90WaitingHours, 1)}
            </td>
            <td class="n">
              {p.medianWorkingHours === null
                ? <span class="too-few" title={`needs ${FOR_MEDIAN} usable calls`}>too few</span>
                : n(p.medianWorkingHours, 1)}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Why most of these cells say "too few"</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A median needs {FOR_MEDIAN} usable calls and a 90th percentile needs {FOR_PERCENTILE}. Below
      those the figure is absent rather than thin. With four calls the median is the mean of the
      middle two and one congested call moves it by hours; a p90 drawn from nine observations is
      the largest of nine, which is a maximum wearing a percentile's name.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both minimums are stated rather than tuned, and the count is always published so you can
      disagree. <strong>"Too few" is a different claim from an uncertain number</strong> — it says
      the figure was never measured, not that it was measured badly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What this sample cannot tell you</h2>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Seven days ({window}) is a short window to call anything normal. The one sample here that
      comfortably supports a median is visibly two populations — a cluster of short calls and a
      tail of long ones — and whether that tail is typical of the port or of one week in September,
      this data cannot say.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exclusions also bias the sample toward shorter visits: a call long enough to span the
      edge of the ingested window is likelier to be truncated, so the longest calls are the ones
      most often dropped. Assume these figures <em>understate</em> waiting times until a longer
      window measures it.
    </p>
    <p>
      And a position in a distribution is not a verdict. Whether a wait was reasonable depends on
      the charter party, the berth and the cargo — none of which AIS can see.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
